 can one solve the problem of Bennett, but in fact prove somewhat more than that. However, in the process of considering these problems, we arrived at a difficult case, whose solution eludes us. This will be elaborated on in the later part of the introduction.\n\n**Theorem 1.1.** *For any odd positive integer \\(c > 1\\), the equation \\(U_k = cz^2 \\pm 1\\) has at most one solution in positive integers \\(k, z\\). In the case \\(c = 1\\), the solution in positive integers \\((k, z) = (3, 2y)\\) to \\(U_k = z^2 - 1\\) exists for all positive integers \\(y\\), and a second solution exists to \\(U_k = z^2 \\pm 1\\) only when \\(y\\) is of the form \\(2t^2 + 2t\\) or \\(2t^2 + 2t + 1\\). In both of these cases, the second solution is given by \\((k, z) = (2, 2t + 1)\\).*\n\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classification: 11B39, 11D25.  \nKey words and phrases: Diophantine equation, linear recurrence sequence.\n\nDOI: 10.4064/aa145-2-3  [129]  © Instytut Matematyczny PAN, 2010--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMath 2177\nName:\nAutumn 2018\nMidterm 1\n09/28/18\nTim (https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/u.osu.edu/dist/2/61147/files/2018/09/Midterm1_Solutions-24m3gcn.pdf)\nciteturn12search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Math 2177 ... 1. (20 points) Consider the function f(x, y) = x2 + 1\nMath 2177\nMidterm 1 - Page 2 of 8\n09/28/18\n1. (20 points) Consider the function f(x, y) = x2 + 1\n2(y2 + y).\n(a) (5 points) Find critical points of the given f(x, y) and classify them. Compute the values\nof f at the critical points.\nSolution:\n2x = 0,\nx = 0,\n(\nfx(x, y) = 0,\n\n\n\n\nfy(x, y) = 0 −→\ny + 1\ny = −1\n\n\n2 = 0\n−→\n2\nThe point (0, −1\n2) is the only critical point of f.\nClassify the type: We compute\nfxx(x, y) = 2,\nfyy(x, y) = 1,\nfxy(x, y) = fyx(x, y) = 0.\nTherefore, the discriminant is equal to\nD(x, y) = fxx(x, y)fyy(x, y) −(fxy(x, y))2 = 2 · 1 −02 = 2.\nIn particular, D(0, −1\n2) = 2 > 0. Moreover, fxx(0, 